{"name":"Oklahoma Wheat","hex":"#f5e0ba","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wheat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the several cereal grains, of the genus Triticum, that yields flour as used in bakery.","A light brown color, like that of wheat."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wheaten, of a light brown color, like that of wheat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wheat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oklahoma","blocks":[{"text":"Oklahoma, is a landlocked state in the South Central, Southern, and Southwestern regions of the United States. It borders Texas to the southwest, Kansas to the north, Missouri to the northeast, Arkansas to the southeast, New Mexico to the west, and Colorado to the northwest. Partially in the western extreme of the Upland South, it is the 20th-most extensive and the 28th-most populous of the 50 United States. Its residents are known as Oklahomans, and its capital and largest city is Oklahoma City."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg/330px-Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oklahoma","wikidata":"Q1649"},{"title":"Wheat","blocks":[{"text":"Wheat is a group of wild and domesticated grasses of the genus Triticum. As cereals, they are cultivated for their grains, which are staple foods around the world. Well-known wheat species and hybrids include the most widely grown common wheat, spelt, durum, emmer, einkorn, and Khorasan or Kamut. The archaeological record suggests that wheat was first cultivated in the regions of the Fertile Crescent around 9600 BC."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Vehn%C3%A4pelto_6.jpg/330px-Vehn%C3%A4pelto_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat","wikidata":"Q15645384"},{"title":"Wheat (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Wheat is a color that resembles the light yellow of the wheat grain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Wheat_grain.jpg/330px-Wheat_grain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat_(color)","wikidata":"Q958363"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/2008_OK_Proof.png/500px-2008_OK_Proof.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/2008_OK_Proof.png/500px-2008_OK_Proof.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2008_OK_Proof.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Former_Indian_Reservations_in_Oklahoma.jpg/500px-Former_Indian_Reservations_in_Oklahoma.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Former_Indian_Reservations_in_Oklahoma.jpg/500px-Former_Indian_Reservations_in_Oklahoma.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian reservations in Oklahoma before the Dawes Act of 1887.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFormer_Indian_Reservations_in_Oklahoma.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg/250px-Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg/500px-Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Farmer_walking_in_dust_s